er.clj:185:(defn declaration-differences\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:194:(defn- structural-correspondence\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:226:(defn- syntax-places\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:240:(defn reconstruct-bindings\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:268:(defn attributed?\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:269:  \"Query one already-scoped fixture's table, bindings and complete lane traces.\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:271:  [{:keys [site-table bindings lanes origin root guest-symbol requested-arity carriers]}]\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:290:    (every? (fn [lane-id]\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:291:              (let [lane (get lanes lane-id)]\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:292:                (and (= :completed (:status lane)) (true? (:complete? lane))\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:293:                     (some eligible? (:executed-calls lane)))))\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:297:(defn check-fixture!\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:299:   establish admission authenticity, executable-image derivations, trace\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:300:   completeness, durable retrieval, or D1 reliance.\"\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:341:(defn fetch-tree!\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:342:  \"Fetch a bounded complete canonical row closure; refuse missing or invalid rows.\"\nsrc/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:373:(defn check-replay-root!\n12:Adopt one language-neutral application-occurrence contract over the existing occurrence relation, one reserved frontend-metadata key, and the already-ruled third trace field. Keep code content, provenance, attribution evidence, and verification results separate. Publish the durable correspondence as an additional addressable record referring to the oracle's evidence **without changing that evidence's format**. Production adoption includes Clojure, TypeScript, PHP, Python, their preludes, and the additional ingress paths inventoried below.\n22:The inspected worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-aaq`, branch `attribution-as-query`, reports HEAD `3eebe844e406ba56bc38a5f4b4a0098d811b5efd`. Its tracked `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1794-1839` still generates two-field body-entry traces and uses the offset accounting at lines 1786–1819. Its `BRIEF-aaq.md:15-21` requires the future inventory, three-field instrumentation, and versioned mechanically derived site tables; it does not define their schema. Untracked `probe/aaq_probe.clj:17-29` uses exploratory map-key paths, not the canonical row-position paths; it is not a published contract. The local dispatch log `collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.glm-5.3.stdout.log:1` reports an unrecognized-model error. This does not establish the status of work elsewhere.\n24:Consequently **the oracle evidence schema's exact identifier, field names, and canonical-source-hash algorithm/profile are unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides from the oracle's accepted record**. This document specifies the envelope around that record and its verification obligations, not an independently invented replacement site-table schema. “OracleEvidence” below means the entire accepted versioned oracle value, verbatim, including its site ids, argument-shape declarations, fixture linkage, trace schema, and version. Before durable implementation is accepted, the inventory must pin its concrete file, commit, schema identifier/version, validator, and derivation entry point. Absence of that pin blocks durable acceptance; it does not block the oracle round.\n79:### 2.5 Ephemeral origin and trace field\n87:;; already signed trace shape:\n112:| Oracle evidence | The oracle owns the new versioned site-table and three-field trace contract. Durable work imports it verbatim. | Two-field evidence is not silently upgraded to three fields. Wrong schema/hash/fixture/lane/site-shape refuses attribution. Preserve old evidence as historical data. |\n115:| Semantic/stack/register execution contracts | Read and validate; no instruction-grammar change specified. Current stamps are `v4`, `b2`, `r2`. | Revise only if implementation changes instruction grammar or execution semantics. A new trace field in a harness stream alone does not revise these. |\n147:| Runtime dispatch and resume: `yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:273-332,493,625-711,776`; `yin/vm/semantic.cljc:102,142,333-380` | Cold/hot application routes, operator/operand frames, linked image attachment and resumption all need occurrence context for traced execution. Native engines and UCF lifting must be audited too; no claim they already preserve caller K. |\n153:| Oracle instrumentation/accounting: `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1786-1855,2341-2405,2432-2468` | Oracle-owned trace migration, offset retirement across the full family, both lanes, refusal pins, gate accounting and evidence regeneration per R5/R6. Audit table derivation independently per R7. |\n165:5. Oracle evidence: new versioned caller-site tables; `slice9_evidence.edn`, `slice9_verification.edn`, `slice9_declarations.edn`, `slice9_unexercised.edn`, `fixture_closure.edn`, gate markdown and captured verification/test outputs; `context.edn`, `kernel_gate.edn`, `manifest.edn` and earlier slice evidence as input/history or regeneration targets according to actual dependency. Every changed trace-bearing field/pin must be listed; blanket rewriting of historical evidence is not required.\n221:### 4.2 Independent reconstruction and discovery\n251:| Production Clojure | Reader/source, all production entry points, publication/load/query and trace fixtures | Same portable source/forms and paths, publication/load/query and trace fixtures | Same | Same; run current reader limitations as explicit refusals, not substitute parsers |\n288:These are measurement requirements and design cost models, **not measured performance claims**. Signed A3 requires a budget impact per frontend, and line 29 requires static/runtime separation. This read-only design has no authority to invent benchmark results. Numeric per-corpus totals remain **unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides by measuring the pinned baseline before tagged runs**.\n297:- `E`: actual callee-entry records, per-lane total bytes and maximum record size; `Q_peak`: peak retained trace records and bytes, dropped/blocked writes and drain schedule.\n298:- Guest instruction counts and host-side work counters; wall compile/expand/project/publish/verify/load/query time; process/heap peak and retained memory, including trace buffers, indexes and pending continuations.\n306:1. **B — baseline:** existing code, trace disabled.\n307:2. **S — static annotation:** C1/provenance production, persistence, loading and query enabled; runtime trace disabled.\n310:Also compare the oracle's existing two-field tracing with T to isolate the extra caller-site field from the original body-entry instrumentation. Report S−B and T−S separately, and the old-trace→tagged-trace delta. If instrumentation itself changes code, keep its canonical image and compile cost separate from side-only annotation. Static C1 must change **zero canonical code bytes/addresses and zero guest instructions** for otherwise identical input; runtime tracing has no such zero-cost claim.\n312:Measure cold and warm compile/load/query separately; use fresh publication caches for cold tests, fixed workload/repetition count and host/version, and publish distribution plus raw samples rather than an unqualified single timing. Report query indexes/build time separately from query execution. Do not call a host trace hook “zero overhead” because guest instruction count did not increase.\n314:### 6.3 Bounds published before any tagged run\n316:The implementation preflight record must contain actual finite numbers (no “TBD”, unlimited trace, or post-run adjustment) for:\n322:| Maximum records E and trace capacity C | Bound accepted trace writes for the fixture, declare C in records AND bytes including worst allowed site-id/path length, and declare drain behavior. No credit from a truncated/overflowed trace. |\n323:| Guest instruction ceiling and added work | Bound trace instructions per event/dispatch under each executed format, plus fixed setup/drain costs. Declare a ceiling for each host/format run before T; report baseline, absolute delta and ratio. |\n325:| Peak process/heap/trace/index/continuation memory | Declare finite ceilings and measurement method; if a host lacks a heap counter, use a named process-memory measure and mark comparability limits. Missing measurement is not zero usage. |\n327:For the oracle specifically, current code uses trace capacity **1,000,000 records**, drains after halt, and allows **4,000,000 guest instructions per C lane** (`latte_oracle_test.clj:1827,1847-1854,2453-2454`). These are the inspected baseline settings, not evidence the tagged run fits. Its own note records 2,024,912 walker and 1,105,792 semantic steps for the older binding fixture at line 2454. Restate both ceilings, the new record byte bound and the occupancy argument before T. If the implementation cannot establish enough capacity under those ceilings, publish revised bounds and their justification before running tagged evidence. Never silently raise fuel to get credit, or let a full trace stream park the fixture and misclassify it as semantic failure.\n344:Every gate compares trace-disabled program outcomes first, then occurrence/provenance correctness, then any attribution. A marker is not proof of execution. These extend the signed oracle battery; they do not replace its seven cases and stale-table diagnostic (`ruling-of-record.md:23-34,64-69`).\n355:| Serialized continuation / migration | Strip ephemeral map-node stamps at canonical/continuation projection as §2.6 requires. If a consuming trace mode promises post-resume attribution, verify the separately carried durable context and its source artifact on restore. Missing context refuses attribution; do not claim migration support by retaining forbidden runtime keys. |\n359:| Stale table and schema | A declared id never observed in a trace produces the recorded diagnostic; unsupported versions, fabricated expansions, extra/conflicting/incomplete marker rows, missing retained inputs and wrong profiles produce distinct failures. Do not silently treat malformed evidence as an honest unexecuted site. |\n361:| Trace completeness | Buffer exhaustion, loss, interrupted run, missing trace lane or partial drains never become positive attribution. Trace-writing infrastructure must not recursively instrument its own bookkeeping into an unbounded trace. |\n363:The relevant runtime paths are real, not hypothetical surface names: walker `apply-function` and operand frames (`ast_walker.cljc:273-332`), park/resume and ready queue (`:214-231,467-473,625-711`), semantic restoration (`semantic.cljc:102-130`), and native `:call/:ffi-call/:resume` lowering (`semantic/derive.cljc:92-102`). Frontend-specific suspension fixtures must use supported operations; unsupported language constructs remain explicit refusals. Continuation replay can produce repeated execution events, so the three-field trace is deliberately insufficient for invocation causality; D1 must not claim more than the oracle's existence-based attribution.\n373:3. Implement common occurrence/side-table production, preservation, verification and publication; retain old artifact loading. Declare numeric run bounds from the pinned baseline before tagged runs.\n382:- **Prelude/host budget is unmeasured:** four-host republication is the broadest artifact obligation. No numeric annotation/trace overhead or passing lane is claimed by this design; preflight must record actual per-frontend bounds.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
