{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n ':kondo|clj-kondo' deps.edn bb.edn; cat collab/yang-clojure/1794670000001-engineer-d1-correspondence-partial.findings.md | head -45; sed -n '2490,2525p' test/yang/clojure_test.clj\"","aggregated_output":"deps.edn:119:           :kondo {:extra-deps {clj-kondo/clj-kondo {:mvn/version \"2026.01.19\"}}\ndeps.edn:120:                   :main-opts [\"-m\" \"clj-kondo.main\"]}\n# D1 correspondence: partial implementation findings\n\nStatus: **incomplete; not accepted and not verified**. No tagged verification run was performed, no companion record or verification result was published, and no relying consumer may treat these structural predicates as verification. No commit made.\n\n## Dispatch-time inventory refresh (partial)\n\n- Worktree `d1-correspondence` at `56cc5ca36855c0d047c11b7658e79cfc27d238e6`; tracked tree was clean at dispatch. The frozen `test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_site_tables.edn` SHA-256 is `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75` before and after this work. Its oracle identity is the EDN map with `:version 1` and per-fixture source hash and site table; this hash is a byte-archive check, not a Jing address.\n- Changed contracts proposed here: only a new, incomplete structural checker for the D1 and recipe closed key sets in `src/cljc/yang/correspondence.cljc`; no existing wire contract is changed. Structural acceptance is not address, schema-semantic, endpoint, coverage, derivation, or reconstruction validation. Its compilation-scoped uniqueness check is intentionally deferred because site integers restart per compilation. It does not provide the recipe profile, persisted inputs, discovery/retention schema, or result publication.\n- Oracle read-only dependencies: `kernel-compile`, `kernel-canonical-source-hash`, `kernel-stamp-sites`, `kernel-site-arguments`, `kernel-row`, `cc3-correspond`, and `cc3-oracle-binding` in `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj`; `src/dev/latte_oracle_tables.clj` delegates to oracle private derivations and is insufficient as independent verification. The checked-in table is historical evidence; current compilation supplies an admission scope and ordinal at admission and retains `:admission-origin`, so the new run must persist those coordinates and its actual input closure, not mint old origins.\n- Occurrence and storage read-only dependencies: `yin.vm/occurrence-origin?`, `occurrences`, `application-occurrences`; `yin.vm.encoder/source-envelope`, `project`, `datom-entity-occurrences`; `dao.jing` canonical encoding, addressing, materialization and verified fetch. Published instruction provenance is outside the manifest in `yin.vm.linker.publish/published-instruction-provenance`. Semantic, stack and register images need their own execution contract and derivation checks; endpoint and multiplicity reconstruction still needs implementation.\n- cc3's test uses the post-port wrapper extraction `[:operands 1 :operands 1 :value]`; its stamped-to-pre-stamp traversal unwraps two applications. These must be independently reproduced and checked against `1794519755000-...reverification...findings.md` item 3, not assumed to be the pre-port three-application form.\n- The design's old `yin.repl.query/link-answer` location is stale: it is now private in `yin.shell.query`. The design's uncertainty about a frozen site-table schema and lack of a published provenance side artifact is historical: both exist at this base. `src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc` still exists. Port exception lowering is continuation-based; this work does not change it.\n- Datom-lane decision: retain cc3's **row-lane-only restriction** for D1. No entity-to-occurrence bridge is claimed. The exception-track ownership registry has not been conclusively established, and no files in the bridge surface were modified. Runtime migration or caller propagation is not claimed.\n- This inventory is not the complete §8 step 2 inventory: all reachable artifact families, their execution/derivation contracts, expansion ownership, discovery and retention owners, and exact registry status remain to be established before acceptance.\n\n## Tests and remaining work\n\nRed first: `clojure -M:test -n yang.correspondence-test` failed with missing `yang.correspondence`; after implementation it passed 1 test, 9 assertions. Direct clj-kondo 2026.08.04 on both new code/test files: 0 errors, 0 warnings. `bb test:sub:clj yang.correspondence` is not a registered subsystem. The full oracle suite was not run.\n\nThe closed structural predicates are merely a partial starting point. The remaining acceptance items include lossless Jing round-trip and retrieval, the exact recipe/profile and retained input closure, a real companion record, address and execution-contract checks, independent AST-derived declarations and bindings, C1/instruction coverage and multiplicity, scoped attribution over both C lanes, and a separate published verifier result. In particular, no independent verifier exists yet. The verifier must reject unknown evidence schema, failed reconstruction and missing retained admission without using `:trusted` as a substitute.\n\nNo D1 numeric preflight was declared or measured. cc3 v2.1's historical maxima (compiled-inclusive retained 8,924,640 B; aggregate serialized 1,169,745 B; peak used heap 272,026,880 B; pipeline 772 ms) are baselines only, not D1 measurements. A finite D1 declaration including trace capacity and bytes, workload, per-phase time, guest fuel and memory must precede any tagged run. No tagged run is authorized by this partial finding.\n(defn run-guest-machine\n  \"Run an AST through either observer composition with a finite instruction\n   budget. Return the terminal machine so fixtures can observe its streams.\"\n  [ast modules lane step-limit]\n  (let [run-bounded (fn [machine]\n                      (loop [v machine, n 0]\n                        (cond\n                          (or (vm/halted? v) (vm/blocked? v)) v\n                          (= n step-limit) (throw (ex-info \"Guest instruction budget exhausted\"\n                                                           {:lane lane :steps n :consumer v}))\n                          :else (recur (vm/step v) (inc n)))))\n        semantic? (= lane :semantic)\n        machine (if semantic?\n                  (semantic/create-vm {:make-stream tu/make-stream\n                                       :capability-secret tu/secret :modules modules})\n                  (tu/create-vm {:modules modules}))\n        session (tu/make-observer-session machine)\n        load-program (if semantic?\n                       (fn [v rows stamp]\n                         (vm/check-contract! vm/ast-contract stamp)\n                         (semantic/load-vector v (:vector (linearize/project-rows rows))\n                                               vm/semantic-contract))\n                       ast-walker/vm-load-program)\n        queued ((if semantic? tu/queue-rows! tu/queue-ast!) session ast)]\n    (-> (observer/run-on-stream queued engine/ready-for-ingress?\n                                (vm/fresh-code-loader load-program vm/ast-contract)\n                                run-bounded)\n        :consumer)))\n\n\n(defn run-guest-machine-depth\n  \"Like run-guest-machine, but records the maximum continuation depth\n   (the semantic lane's K-vector length, or the walker lane's :next chain\n   length) across the run, and returns the terminal machine with the max\n   depth attached under `:max-depth`.\"\n  [ast modules lane step-limit]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
